---
import type { Source } from '../types/course';
// Verification ledger for any section carrying a government fact. Two columns:
//   Verified 24 Jun 2026 | Deposit caps fact-checked
//                          against QBCC Act 1991, Schedule 1B
// The date sits on the left; the fact and its linked source(s) on the right. Always visible,
// source links crawlable for E-E-A-T. No JavaScript.
interface Props { date: string; facts?: string; sources: Source[]; }
const { date, facts, sources } = Astro.props;
const hasSources = sources.length > 0;

/* THE JOINER IS CONDITIONAL, and this is the whole point of the component's 29 Jul 2026 change.
   It used to append " against " unconditionally. But `facts` is authored copy, and the house
   phrasing for it is "... fact-checked against the current guidance" — so on 39 of the 69 blocks
   across 7 built pages the page rendered a DOUBLED "against":

     "...the $11,000 permit threshold fact-checked against the current guidance
      against QBCC — Required courses for owner builders"

   It read as a stutter to every reader of those pages, and it was live for as long as the pages
   have been. This was first found by an independent Stage 7 audit of /white-card-wa as F14, fixed
   there by rewriting the copy, and only then measured across the site.

   Fixing it page by page was rejected deliberately: 37 hand edits would have left the next page
   free to reintroduce it, because nothing would have stopped it. The component owns its own joiner,
   so the component is where the rule belongs.

   Why a SEPARATOR and not simply dropping the joiner. Dropping it yields "...the current guidance
   QBCC — Required courses", two noun phrases collided with no boundary at all, which is worse than
   the stutter. `·` is the house separator already doing this job in Hero eyebrows, Credentials,
   PartnerDisclosure and the styleguide's own prop lists.

   The test is `\bagainst\b` anywhere in `facts`, not just at the end. A string like "checked
   against the register and the regulator's guidance" is equally doubled, and a word-boundary test
   keeps it away from words that merely contain the letters. Blocks whose facts is a plain noun
   phrase — 30 of the 69 — are untouched and still read "Deposit caps fact-checked against QBCC Act
   1991", which is the reading the component was designed for. */
/* The space before the middot is NON-BREAKING, and this was measured, not assumed. With an ordinary
   space, one of the eight blocks on /qld-owner-builder-course wrapped so the middot began a visual
   line at 375px, reading as a stray dot rather than a separator. A no-break space glues it to the
   last word of `facts`; the space after it stays ordinary, so the line may still break between the
   dot and the source. A bare word like "against" cannot orphan this way, so it is left alone. */
const joiner = facts && /\bagainst\b/i.test(facts) ? ' · ' : ' against ';
---
<div class="verified">
  <span class="v-date">
    <span class="v-label">
      <svg class="check" viewBox="0 0 12 12" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
        <path d="M1.5 6.2 4.4 9 10.5 2.8" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
      </svg>
      Verified
    </span>
    <span class="v-day">{date}</span>
  </span>
  <span class="v-body">
    {facts}{facts && hasSources && <span class="v-join">{joiner}</span>}
    {sources.map((s, i) => (
      <>{i > 0 && ', '}{s.href ? <a href={s.href}>{s.label}</a> : s.label}</>
    ))}
  </span>
</div>
